## Why This Is Asked
Tests familiarity with SwiftUI basics, timer handling, accessibility, and simple persistence in a beginner-friendly scenario.

## Key Concepts
- SwiftUI TimerPublisher and state management
- MVVM pattern with @ObservableObject and @Published
- UserDefaults for minimal persistence
- Accessibility: VoiceOver labels, Dynamic Type, color contrast
- Debouncing user input to prevent thrashing

## Code Example
```swift
class FocusTimerViewModel: ObservableObject {
  @Published var remaining: Int = 0
  @Published var isRunning: Bool = false
  private var cancellable: AnyCancellable?
  func start(duration: Int) {
    remaining = duration * 60
    isRunning = true
    cancellable = Timer.publish(every: 1, on: .main, in: .common)
      .autoconnect()
      .sink { [weak self] _ in
        guard let self = self else { return }
        if self.remaining > 0 { self.remaining -= 1 } else { self.isRunning = false }
      }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle timer accuracy when the app is backgrounded?
- How would you scale to more than 3 presets and persist per-preset state?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an offline-first image pipeline with robust caching, memory management, and accessibility in a real UI scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Two-tier caching (memory + disk)
- LRU eviction under memory pressure
- Prefetching and cancellation tied to visible cells
- Accessibility: Dynamic Type and VoiceOver labels
- Async/await data loading and main-thread UI updates

## Code Example
```swift
import UIKit

actor ImageLoader {
    private let memCache = NSCache<NSString, UIImage>()
    private let fileManager = FileManager.default
    private let diskDir: URL

    init(diskFolder: URL) { self.diskDir = diskFolder }

    func load(from url: URL) async -> UIImage? {
        let key = url.absoluteString as NSString
        if let image = memCache.object(forKey: key) { return image }
        let path = diskDir.appendingPathComponent(url.lastPathComponent)
        if let data = try? Data(contentsOf: path), let image = UIImage(data: data) {
            memCache.setObject(image, forKey: key)
            return image
        }
        guard let (data, _) = try? await URLSession.shared.data(from: url),
              let image = UIImage(data: data) else { return nil }
        memCache.setObject(image, forKey: key)
        try? data.write(to: path)
        return image
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical local persistence, SwiftUI queries, and basic user experience actions without requiring remote data.

## Key Concepts
- Codable persistence to the local Documents directory
- File-based store with load/save and proper error handling
- SwiftUI List with searchable and dynamic filtering by multiple fields
- Row actions: copy to clipboard and share sheet
- Basic test plan and empty-state handling

## Code Example
```swift
struct Snippet: Codable, Identifiable {
    let id: UUID
    var title: String
    var content: String
    var tag: String
    var createdDate: Date
}

class SnippetStore: ObservableObject {
    @Published var snippets: [Snippet] = []

    private let fileName = "snippets.json"

    private var fileURL: URL {
        FileManager.default.urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask)[0]
            .appendingPathComponent(fileName)
    }

    func load() {
        guard FileManager.default.fileExists(atPath: fileURL.path) else { return }
        do {
            let data = try Data(contentsOf: fileURL)
            snippets = try JSONDecoder().decode([Snippet].self, from: data)
        } catch {
            print("Failed to load: \(error)")
        }
    }

    func save() {
        do {
            let data = try JSONEncoder().encode(snippets)
            try data.write(to: fileURL)
        } catch {
            print("Failed to save: \(error)")
        }
    }
}

struct ContentView: View {
    @StateObject private var store = SnippetStore()
    @State private var searchText = ""

    var filteredSnippets: [Snippet] {
        if searchText.isEmpty { return store.snippets }
        let query = searchText.lowercased()
        return store.snippets.filter {
            $0.title.lowercased().contains(query) ||
            $0.content.lowercased().contains(query) ||
            $0.tag.lowercased().contains(query)
        }
    }

    var body: some View {
        NavigationStack {
            Group {
                if store.snippets.isEmpty {
                    ContentUnavailableView(
                        "No Snippets",
                        systemImage: "doc.text",
                        description: Text("Add your first snippet to get started")
                    )
                } else {
                    List(filteredSnippets) { snippet in
                        VStack(alignment: .leading) {
                            Text(snippet.title).font(.headline)
                            Text(snippet.content).lineLimit(2).foregroundStyle(.secondary)
                            Text(snippet.tag).font(.caption).padding(4).background(Color.blue.opacity(0.2)).cornerRadius(4)
                        }
                        .contextMenu {
                            Button("Copy") {
                                UIPasteboard.general.string = snippet.content
                            }
                            ShareLink(item: snippet.content)
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            .navigationTitle("SnippetBook")
            .searchable(text: $searchText, prompt: "Search snippets")
            .onAppear { store.load() }
        }
    }
}
```

## Test Plan
1. **Persistence Test**: Launch app, add snippets, kill app, relaunch — snippets should persist
2. **Search Test**: Type in search field — results should filter by title, content, or tag
3. **Empty State Test**: Launch fresh — empty state should display
4. **Actions Test**: Long-press row — copy and share options should work

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses offline-first design, delta-based syncing, and conflict handling under intermittent connectivity, which are critical in collaboration apps used by teams.

## Key Concepts

- Core Data data model for Comment and Patch objects
- Delta/patch format including baseVersion, changes, and timestamps
- Reconciliation strategy (latest-writer-wins or server-validated merges)
- Robust test plan for offline edits, re-connect, and concurrency

## Code Example

```javascript
// Swift-like pseudocode: apply a patch to a Comment
func apply(patch: Patch, to comment: inout Comment) {
    // Validate base version matches current state
    guard patch.baseVersion == comment.version else {
        // Handle conflict resolution
        resolveConflict(patch: patch, comment: &comment)
        return
    }

    // Apply delta changes
    for change in patch.changes {
        switch change.type {
        case .insert:
            comment.text.insert(change.text, at: change.position)
        case .delete:
            comment.text.removeSubrange(change.range)
        case .replace:
            comment.text.replaceSubrange(change.range, with: change.text)
        }
    }

    // Update version and timestamps
    comment.version += 1
    comment.updatedAt = Date()
}
```
